
  [1mLast fork before I present the design: how logs get correlated back to a user.[0m A pile of independent error records is
  much harder to debug than a thread of events from one session, but the identifier that makes correlation work is also
  the part that carries PII to the vendor.

  - [1mAnonymous session ID[0m — a random UUID per browser session ([38;5;153msessionStorage[39m) or per process run in Node, attached to
    every record. Threads one session's events together with no personal data leaving the browser. You can't connect two
    visits by the same person, and when a user emails "it broke for me at 3pm" you have to find them by timestamp and
    symptom.
  - [1mPersistent anonymous device ID[0m — a UUID in [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m that survives sessions. Correlates repeat failures from the
    same browser, which is how you spot "this only breaks for people with a stale cached token." It's durable
    cross-session tracking of an individual, so it likely needs a consent/privacy review depending on your jurisdiction.
  - [1mSession ID plus an opt-in [38;5;153msetUser(id)[39m hook[0m — anonymous by default, with an explicit call after successful login that
    attaches an opaque internal user ID (never the username or email from that form). Direct answer to "what happened
    to this specific user," and stays off by default until you decide to call it.

  Note on all three: whatever you pick has to pass the allowlist we just agreed on, and [38;5;153musername[39m from the login form
  should not be the identifier — it's user-supplied PII headed to a third party.

  My recommendation is the third. Anonymous session ID as the default gets you the threading that makes logs debuggable,
  and the [38;5;153msetUser[39m hook is a single call site you can leave uncalled until the privacy question is settled — you're not
  committing to sending user IDs by building the hook.
